    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3216.0,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 721,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two types of atoms: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the literal basis for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at its unique properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique Physical Properties of Water\nWater is famous among scientists because it behaves very differently from most other liquids on Earth. \n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of the electrical polarity of its molecules (the oxygen side is slightly negative, and the hydrogen sides are slightly positive), water attracts and dissolves more substances than any other liquid. This is why things like salt, sugar, and vitamins dissolve easily in it.\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. Because of this, lakes and oceans freeze from the top down, insulating the water below and allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This unique ability allows the oceans to regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas. \n* **The Distribution:** Roughly **96.5%** of Earth's water is salt water found in the oceans. Of the remaining 3.5% that is fresh water, most is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Only a tiny fraction (less than 1%) is easily accessible in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is never lost; it is constantly recycled through the **hydrologic (water) cycle**. The sun heats water, causing it to evaporate into the air as vapor. It cools and condenses to form clouds, and eventually falls back to Earth as rain, snow, or hail (precipitation), flowing back into rivers and oceans.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife on Earth likely began in the oceans billions of years ago, and every living thing still depends on water today.\n* **Biological Necessity:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Water acts as a transporter of nutrients and oxygen in the blood, regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and flushes out waste. Without water, human cells cannot function, and death occurs within a few days.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species of plants and animals, ranging from microscopic plankton to the blue whale, the largest animal on Earth.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is a powerful solvent, a climate regulator, a global cycle, and the essential lifeblood of every living organism on our planet."
